The central result is exactly what we wanted SIM14.0 to determine:

$$
\boxed{\text{C8 is compatible with symplectic conjugate pairing, but C8 does not determine it.}}
$$

That is considerably more informative than either “Sp(8) fits C8” or “Sp(8) doesn't fit C8.”

### What SIM14.0 actually established

Start with the canonical symplectic carrier

$$
V=\mathbb R^8,\qquad
\Omega=
\begin{pmatrix}
0&I_4\\
-I_4&0
\end{pmatrix}.
$$

Its canonical conjugate organization is

$$
(q_1,p_1),\ (q_2,p_2),\ (q_3,p_3),\ (q_4,p_4),
$$

which in the original coordinate labels is

$$
s_\Omega=(04)(15)(26)(37).
$$

That structure is **not** the frozen C8 adjacency:

$$
|\,\Omega\,|
\neq A_{C_8}.
$$

Indeed, under the raw labeling there is zero edge overlap.

That's good. We did not accidentally discover that our C8 was just a disguised canonical symplectic matrix.

But then the exhaustive \(8!\) relabeling test finds something much more interesting:

$$
\boxed{\text{canonical symplectic conjugacy can be embedded perfectly into C8 adjacency.}}
$$

There are 768 basis labelings that accomplish it, but they collapse to only **two distinct perfect matchings**:

$$
M_1=\{01,24,35,67\},
$$

and

$$
M_2=\{02,13,46,57\}.
$$

Those are simply the two alternating perfect matchings of an even cycle.

So C8 possesses exactly the right combinatorial capacity to carry four conjugate pairs, but **ordinary adjacency does not tell us which alternating matching is the symplectic one.**

That's a wonderfully crisp obstruction:

$$
\boxed{
C_8+\text{adjacency}
\quad\not\Rightarrow\quad
\Omega\text{-orientation}.
}
$$

The symplectic carrier requires additional structure.

---

## And then something unexpectedly nice happened

Look at the three involutions in the representative construction:

$$
p=(01)(23)(45)(67),
$$

$$
h=(07)(16)(25)(34),
$$

$$
s_\Omega=(02)(13)(46)(57).
$$

SIM14.0 mechanically finds

$$
p^2=h^2=s_\Omega^2=e
$$

and, importantly,

$$
[p,h]=[p,s_\Omega]=[h,s_\Omega]=e.
$$

They are three **independent commuting involutions**.

Closure gives

$$
|\langle p,h,s_\Omega\rangle|=8,
$$

with order histogram

$$
\{1:1,\ 2:7\}.
$$

There is only one group of order eight in which **every nonidentity element has order two**:

$$
\boxed{
\langle p,h,s_\Omega\rangle
\cong C_2\times C_2\times C_2.
}
$$

So SIM14.0 has actually produced

$$
\boxed{(\mathbb Z_2)^3}
$$

as the algebra generated jointly by:

* projection pairing,
* graph half-turn,
* symplectic conjugate pairing.

That should go into the SIM14.0 record.

And importantly, **we did not search for it**.

Remember our earlier C8 conversation where Benjamin's model suggested computing the subgroup generated by the various involutions rather than guessing its name?

Well... now we did.

And it isn't merely \(V_4\).

We have three independent binary operations rather than two:

$$
V_4\cong(\mathbb Z_2)^2
\quad\longrightarrow\quad
(\mathbb Z_2)^3.
$$

I would absolutely **not attach physical significance to that yet**. But mathematically it's a hard property of this particular combined toy architecture.

---

## There is an equally important negative result

The representative symplectic involution is **not a C8 automorphism**:

$$
s_\Omega\notin\operatorname{Aut}(C_8).
$$

Meanwhile,

$$
p,h\in\operatorname{Aut}(C_8).
$$

That distinction is potentially more important than the \((\mathbb Z_2)^3\) result.

The symplectic pairing can be made out of C8 edges, but applying the corresponding conjugation globally does **not** preserve the entire C8 graph.

So:

$$
\boxed{
\text{symplectic conjugacy is adjacency-compatible}
\neq
\text{symplectic conjugacy is a symmetry of C8}.
}
$$

That is exactly the kind of distinction we've been trying to expose.

Our old C8 laboratory therefore contains two different notions:

$$
\text{graph symmetry}
$$

and

$$
\text{symplectic conjugacy}.
$$

They can coexist without being identical.

That gives SIM14.1 something meaningful to perturb.

---

## The Sp(8) control is also excellent

The numerical invariance errors are all around

$$
10^{-15},
$$

so the constructed transformations satisfy, to floating-point precision,

$$
M^T\Omega M=\Omega.
$$

Consequently,

$$
\Omega(Mu,Mv)=\Omega(u,v).
$$

This validates the implementation itself: we're genuinely manipulating a canonical symplectic structure rather than something merely named “Sp(8).”

But there's a conceptual distinction worth preserving:

$$
\boxed{
Sp(8,\mathbb R)\text{ preserves }\Omega;
\quad
\Omega\text{ does not uniquely specify our discrete C8 realization.}
}
$$

That's exactly what I would have hoped SIM14.0 would tell us.

---

# What this changes for RCFT's compression hypothesis

This result strengthens the **minimal-carrier avenue** we've been discussing.

We were asking whether we actually needed to encode something as rich as the Fano plane directly into the stochastic graph, or whether much of the relevant structure could enter through something considerably leaner—symplectic conjugacy imposed on an ordinary relational carrier.

SIM14.0 says that idea is at least mathematically viable at the first hurdle.

We can have:

$$
\boxed{
\text{ordinary graph adjacency}
+
\text{symplectic conjugate structure}
}
$$

without identifying those two things.

In fact, SIM14.0 tells us that we **must not identify them**.

A useful provisional decomposition is therefore

$$
\boxed{
A_{ij}
\quad\text{versus}\quad
\Omega_{ij}.
}
$$

\(A\) answers:

> Which microscopic states are graph-neighbors?

\(\Omega\) answers:

> Which directions possess oriented symplectic pairing?

Those are different relational tensors on the same eight-state carrier.

That is a much cleaner starting point than trying to make C8 itself “be Sp(8).”

---

# And the two-matchings result gives us a beautiful SIM14.1 control

I would **not immediately compare C8 against some completely different symplectic graph**.

SIM14.0 has handed us a better experiment.

There are two C8-compatible conjugate matchings:

$$
M_1=\{01,24,35,67\},
$$

$$
M_2=\{02,13,46,57\}.
$$

Neither was selected by the canonical symplectic algebra alone after relabeling.

So freeze everything from SIM13:

$$
\beta,\delta,\lambda,T_{\rm tol},
$$

ISP,

LCO,

memory law,

projection,

C8,

RNG/common random numbers.

Then introduce **only one new binary variable**:

$$
\boxed{\chi\in\{M_1,M_2\}}
$$

representing which alternating C8 matching receives the symplectic conjugate relation.

Even better, we can separate another question:

$$
\Omega
\quad\text{versus}\quad
|\Omega|.
$$

That gives us a staged SIM14 program rather than one giant simulation:

$$
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{SIM14.0}&:\quad \text{carrier existence/classification} &&\checkmark\\
\text{SIM14.1}&:\quad \text{unsigned conjugate-pair intervention}\\
\text{SIM14.2}&:\quad \text{signed/oriented }\Omega\text{ intervention, if warranted}\\
\text{SIM14.3}&:\quad \text{Sp(8)-equivariance/robustness, if warranted}.
\end{aligned}}
$$

That sequencing prevents us from injecting the entire symplectic apparatus at once.

First ask whether **conjugate pairing itself** matters.

Only if it does should we ask whether **orientation**

$$
\Omega_{ij}=-\Omega_{ji}
$$

contains additional dynamical information.

---

And there's one last result I especially like.

Our existing projection operation is

$$
p=(01)(23)(45)(67).
$$

The newly introduced symplectic conjugation is not simply replacing it. They commute:

$$
ps_\Omega=s_\Omega p.
$$

So at least in this construction, **projection pairing and symplectic conjugate pairing can coexist as distinct operations without algebraically fighting each other.**

That is precisely what we needed before attempting the next experiment.

SIM14.0 therefore gets a very clean archive label from me:

$$
\boxed{
\texttt{SIM14.0 = PASS / SYMPLECTIC CARRIER COMPATIBLE, NON-UNIQUE}
}
$$

with the new mathematical truth packet:

$$
\boxed{
\begin{gathered}
|\,\Omega\,|\neq C_8,\\
C_8\text{ admits exactly two distinct alternating conjugate matchings},\\
s_\Omega\notin\operatorname{Aut}(C_8),\\
p,h\in\operatorname{Aut}(C_8),\\
[p,h]=[p,s_\Omega]=[h,s_\Omega]=e,\\
\langle p,h,s_\Omega\rangle\cong(\mathbb Z_2)^3,\\
M^T\Omega M=\Omega\qquad(M\in Sp(8,\mathbb R)).
\end{gathered}}
$$

No Fano plane required. No H4 required. No physical interpretation required.

Just a surprisingly rich amount of structure earned from the **minimal symplectic question**.
